<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ganga Singh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1880">1880</a>–<a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ganga Singh was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Bikaner from 1887 to 1943 and one of the most prominent Indian princes of his time. He modernised his desert state, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gang-canal">Gang Canal</a> to irrigate its north and expanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganga-risala">Ganga Risala</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> corps. He sat in the Imperial War Cabinet during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and was the first chancellor of the Chamber of Princes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ganga-singh.jpg" alt="Ganga Singh" width="320" height="486" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Vandyk · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ganga_Singh_c1930.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ganga Singh was born on 13 October 1880, the day of Vijay Dashmi, to Maharaj Shri Lal Singh Sahib and Maji Shri Chandravatiji Sahiba, of the royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> family of Bikaner, and succeeded his brother Dungar Singh as a boy. He was taught first by Pandit Ram Chandra Dube and for five years at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayo-college">Mayo College</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a>. In 1894 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-bruce-8th-earl-of-elgin">Lord Elgin</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy of India</a>, chose Brian Egerton as his guardian and tutor to further British interests in Bikaner, a post Egerton held until 1899. In 1898 he went for military training to Deoli, attached to the 42nd Deoli Regiment.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boxer-rebellion">Boxer Rebellion</a> of 1900 and was an honorary aide-de-camp to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>, later King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he commanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganga-risala">Bikaner Camel Corps</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, and he represented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> at the Imperial War Conference of 1917 and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> of 1919. He was Chancellor of the Chamber of Princes from 1920 to 1926 and a delegate to the fifth session of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> in 1924.</p><p>At home he announced a Representative Assembly in 1913 and in 1922 set up a High Court with a Chief Justice and two judges, the first in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana-agency">Rajputana Agency</a>. He ended child marriage by law, brought railways and electricity to the state, reformed the prisons, built the Lalgarh Palace between 1902 and 1926 and founded schools and colleges, especially for women. After the famine of 1899 and 1900 he built a canal bringing water from the Satluj, which drew settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and made Sri Ganganagar a grain bowl.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> on 2 February 1943, aged 62, after a reign of 56 years, and was succeeded by his son Sadul Singh. In 2003 the University of Bikaner was renamed Maharaja Ganga Singh University.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ganga Singh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Maharaja <b>Ganga Singh</b> (r. 1887–1943) raised the Ganga Risala camel corps, sat in the Imperial War Cabinet during the First World War and built the Ganga Canal, opened in 1927 to irrigate the north of the state.</blockquote><a href="/state/bikaner-state">Bikaner State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Bikaner State. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.08,72.88&amp;year=1920" aria-label="Mumbai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6521 1457 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ganga Singh"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6971 1727h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6971 1727h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=19.08,72.88&amp;year=1920">What was here in 1920? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
